  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Summary: From Four Dimensions to Two</H2>
    <Body>
The FitzHugh-Nagumo model proves that you don't need four coupled differential equations to understand how neurons generate action potentials. Two variables — one fast, one slow — are enough to capture:

- **Excitability**: stable rest, but large response to suprathreshold input
- **All-or-none spiking**: small changes in stimulus near threshold produce qualitatively different responses
- **Refractory period**: the slow recovery variable prevents immediate re-firing
- **Transition to oscillations**: Hopf bifurcation from excitable to autonomous spiking

The geometric tools you've learned here — nullcline analysis, fixed point classification, eigenvalue stability — extend far beyond this specific model. They're the language of nonlinear dynamics in neuroscience.

Every time you sketch a phase plane, you're compressing infinite possible trajectories into a single visual summary. That's the power of low-dimensional reductions: they reveal the *structure* hidden inside complex systems.
    </Body>
  </Section>

</Lesson>
